  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/pimephales_promelas_wild_colored_gold_en/",
      "html": "<p>This tiny species, hardly 10 cm long, originates from North America. Its overall coloration is similar to that of the European minnow (Phoxinus phoxinus) in its eclipse plumage, hence the popular name \"Fathead minnow\".</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/pimephales_promelas_wild_colored_gold_en/",
      "html": "<p>As Pimephales promelas is widely used as bait, it can be found in most parts of North America.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/pimephales_promelas_wild_colored_gold_en/",
      "html": "<p>While the European minnow is a stenoecious fish that needs cool and oxygen-rich environments, the fathead minnow can be found in a rich variety of habitats. The fish can be found as well in cool and clear springs as in muddy and warm pools. Water temperature may be between 4 and over 30°C.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/pimephales_promelas_wild_colored_gold_en/",
      "html": "<p>The fathead minnow is one of the most undemanding coldwater fishes at all, and a very interesting and easy to keep aquarium fish. This opportunism makes it an ideal fish for small garden ponds. The golden sport of the fish is in fact the only \"goldfish\" that can be kept with a good conscience in such miniature pools, where the fish is very useful as it feeds on mosquito larvae.</p><p>In regions with winter temperatures below 0°C the fish have to be hibernated in a cool and dark, but frost-free bucket or aquarium. In large ponds that are deep enough and do not freeze to the bottom in winter, the hardy fish can also hibernate in the pond.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/pimephales_promelas_wild_colored_gold_en/",
      "html": "<p>Males develop odd stingy warts during the breeding season and become almost black. The species belongs to the rare cyprinids that care for the brood. The eggs are laid under or on roots, stones etc. and the male guards them until the fry swims free. Thus it seems very likely that the warts on the head are somehow connected with brood care. Maybe the males use them in a similar manner against potential egg-feeders as cattle do with their horns against a potential aggressor.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/pimephales_promelas_wild_colored_gold_en/",
      "html": "<p>Lexicon: Pimephales: abbreviation of the ancient Greek Pimelecephales, which means \"fat head\". promelas: from ancient Greek, means \"black ahead\". Phoxinus: a name used for a river fish in ancient Greece; it is not identifiable nowadays anymore.</p>"
    }
  ],
